# File SQL implementation notes (decisions / failed attempts)
#
# Porting model
# - Reused the existing multimode table function infrastructure (JSON_TABLE plan node +
#   ObJsonTableSpec) instead of adding a new logical operator. read_csv/read_json/
#   read_parquet/glob all resolve into an ObJsonTableOp with a File SQL table type and a
#   per-query schema. This keeps the optimizer, CG and executor untouched except for the
#   small table-type checks.
# - Streaming is mandatory: FileSqlScan reads through a 64 KiB std::streambuf for CSV/JSONL
#   and a page-at-a-time pread reader for Parquet. No file is fully materialized; oversized
#   records fail explicitly. Glob only materializes the path list.
#
# Security
# - Every path goes through realpath + ObResolverUtils::check_secure_path, and the opened
#   descriptor is re-validated via /proc/self/fd to close the symlink race. Files must be
#   regular files and are opened O_NOFOLLOW. COPY reuses ObSelectIntoOp's secure path check.
# - A (st_dev, st_ino, size, mtime, ctime) fingerprint is captured during schema inference
#   and re-checked at EOF so the data cannot change between DESCRIBE and scan.
#
# Parquet reader (new, no third-party library)
# - Hand-written Thrift compact-protocol reader + raw Snappy decompressor.
# - Flat, non-repeated schemas only (nested/repeated is rejected with 1235).
# - DataPage v1/v2, PLAIN and RLE_DICTIONARY encodings, INT32/INT64/INT96/FLOAT/DOUBLE/
#   BOOLEAN/BYTE_ARRAY/FIXED; DATE/TIMESTAMP logical or converted types are rendered as text
#   and re-parsed by the shared FileSqlScan conversion path.
# - Each column chunk keeps its own page cursor (pread), so rows are produced one at a time
#   across columns without buffering a row group.
#
# Failed attempts / issues found while integrating
# - Thrift compact protocol field ids are deltas relative to the *current struct*; the first
#   implementation forgot to save/restore the enclosing struct's last-id around nested
#   structs and skips, which shifted every later field id (footer parse failed with -4070).
#   Fixed by saving/restoring last-id at every nested parse and inside Compact::skip.
# - Parquet footer length is stored *before* the trailing "PAR1" magic; the first version
#   read them in the opposite order.
# - DataPageV2 keeps levels uncompressed and only compresses the values section; the level
#   RLE run must be consumed lazily (only the bytes needed for num_values), otherwise the
#   first bytes of the Snappy stream are eaten.
# - DESCRIBE SELECT cannot reuse parse_node.value_ as a marker: the statement-level
#   question_mark_issue() requires value_ == INT64_MAX and aborts with -4016. The describe
#   marker now lives in the EXPLAIN format slot (T_INT 1) and is ignored for plain EXPLAIN.
# - COPY must attach its options node to the SELECT's PARSE_SELECT_INTO_EXTRA slot; that
#   slot only dispatches to resolve_into_outfile_with_format for T_EXTERNAL_FILE_FORMAT,
#   so T_FILE_SQL_COPY_OPTIONS had to be added to that condition. Otherwise the resolver
#   falls into the legacy 5-child path and returns -4016.
# - COPY header output: ObSelectIntoOp had no header support. Added with_header_ +
#   header_names_ (+ nullstr) to ObSelectIntoItem -> ObLogSelectInto -> ObSelectIntoSpec ->
#   ObSelectIntoOp, defaulted off so SELECT INTO OUTFILE behavior is unchanged.
# - The official fixture include copies data with ssh/scp; local verification used
#   path-local stand-ins for ssh/scp. The stand-in must copy sources->last-argument,
#   otherwise it overwrites a fixture (this actually corrupted the checked-in basic.jsonl
#   once; it was restored with `git checkout -- tools/.../file_sql/data`).
#
# Known gaps
# - Parquet: no GZIP/ZSTD/BROTLI/LZO codecs, no DELTA_* / BYTE_STREAM_SPLIT encodings,
#   no nested/repeated columns, no INT96 timezone semantics beyond UTC-normalized text.
# - CSV: no compression, no type-override options (only header/sample_size per contract).
# - JSONL: objects must be newline-delimited objects (contract format), no arrays.
# - DESCRIBE metadata is generated by re-parsing a bounded literal SELECT; it does not
#   execute any file data.
